<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <!--
      Not "FuzzyRegex.AotSmoke", although that is the folder name: a namespace beginning
      "FuzzyRegex" shadows the FuzzyRegex type this app exists to call.
    -->
    <RootNamespace>FuzzyRegexSamples.AotSmoke</RootNamespace>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>

    <!--
      S53, the consumer half of the AOT gate. tools/run-aot-tests.ps1 publishes the whole test
      suite natively, which is the stronger evidence; this app answers what only a separate
      consumer can. It reaches the library through a PROJECT REFERENCE from outside the test
      tree, it roots nothing, and it trims as a real application would, so the binary size and
      startup time it reports are the numbers a user would see - Phase 7's baseline.

      PublishAot is NOT set here. It is passed by tools/run-aot-smoke.ps1 on the publish command
      line, exactly as the test project does it, and that is load-bearing rather than tidiness:
      setting it in the csproj makes the SDK write the matching feature switch into the ORDINARY
      build's runtimeconfig too, so `RuntimeFeature.IsDynamicCodeSupported` reads false under a
      plain `dotnet run` and this app's own "which runtime am I on" line reported a Native AOT run
      when there had not been one. S53's blind review caught that; keeping the switch on the
      publish alone is what makes the report true.
    -->

    <!--
      No IL2xxx or IL3xxx suppression here, and none in src/FuzzyRegex either: the point of this
      project is that a consumer publishing the library gets a clean ILCompiler run. If a trim
      warning ever appears, the fix belongs in src/, test-first, never in a [DynamicDependency]
      band-aid here.
    -->
    <InvariantGlobalization>true</InvariantGlobalization>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../../src/FuzzyRegex/FuzzyRegex.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
